In [[Balisca]], '''''Mestizo''''' ({{Literal translation|mixed}}), is a cultural and [[W:Panethnic|panethnic]] term used by the [[Instituto Confederal de Stadística]] (ICS) in the Baliscan census. The term "''mestizo''" is a complex and sometimes confusing one, although it is considered a blanket, neutral term for someone of [[W:Multinational state#Definition|mixed ethnic ancestries]].

As a result of the confluence and standardization of [[Baliscano language|Baliscano]] in the [[Cosmocazo|post-''Cosmocazo'']], terms such as "[[W:Casta|casta]]" (and the derived "[[W:Castizo|castizo]]"), "[[W:Mulatto|mulatto]]", and "[[W:Pardo|pardo]]" were gradually abolished. Mestizo Baliscans represent a diverse range of [[W:Human skin color|skin colors]] and ethnic backgrounds. 

The equivalent terms used in [[W:Mapuche language|Walldungu]] and the [[Kurupiran languages]] are ''Txipantü Küpal'' ({{Literal translation|mixed lineage}}) and ''Hãgá-monhang'' ({{Literal translation|created lineage}}), respectively. 

